.\" trash-restore(1) manual page for trash-cli.
.TH TRASH-RESTORE 1 2026-11-14 "trash-cli 0.26.9.29" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash\-restore \- restore a trashed file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash\-restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
[\fIpath\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash\-restore
lists the trashed files whose original location was
.I path
or lay under it, and asks which of them to restore.
Without a
.I path
argument the current directory is used; a relative
.I path
is resolved against the current directory.
A trashed file is a candidate when its original location equals
.I path
or lies under it;
a
.I path
of
\fB/\fR
matches everything, so a directory argument lists everything trashed from
its whole subtree.
.PP
The candidates are listed, one line per entry with the index right\-aligned
in four columns and the raw deletion date
(\fBNone\fR
when the trashinfo file has no valid date), followed by the prompt
\fBWhat file to restore [0..N]: \fR
(the index range runs from 0 to the number of candidates minus 1, and the
prompt line ends with a space):
.RS
.PP
.nf
   0 2026\-11\-13 19:51:08 /home/user/work/rf1
   1 2026\-11\-13 19:51:08 /home/user/work/rf2
What file to restore [0..1]:\ 
.fi
.RE
.PP
The reply is a comma\-separated list of indices or inclusive ranges:
\fB0\-2, 3\fR
restores indices 0, 1, 2 and 3; spaces around the commas are tolerated.
An empty line restores nothing:
\fBNo files were restored\fR
is printed and the exit status is 0.
An invalid reply is reported once on standard error and the exit status is
1:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Invalid entry: not an index: <text>
Invalid entry: open interval: <text>
Invalid entry: out of range 0..N\-1: <index>
.fi
.RE
An end of file at the prompt prints a bare newline on standard
error and exits 1.
.PP
Restoring refuses to overwrite an existing file unless
\fB\-\-overwrite\fR
is given; an existing directory is never overwritten.
The refusal message is
\fBRefusing to overwrite existing file \(dq<basename>\(dq.\fR
and the entry stays in the trash.
Missing parent directories of the destination are created.
The backup copy is moved back and its
.I .trashinfo
file is removed afterwards.
Several indices may be given; a failure does not abort the batch, but any
failure makes the exit status 1.
.PP
When there are no candidates,
\fBNo files trashed from current dir (\(aq<realpath of cwd>\(aq)\fR
is printed when no
.I path
argument was given, and
\fBNo files trashed from \(aq<path>\(aq\fR
when one was; the exit status is 0.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-completion {\fIbash\fR,\fIzsh\fR,\fItcsh\fR}
Print a completion script for the named shell and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program name and version, for example
\fBtrash\-restore 0.26.9.29\fR,
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-sort {\fIdate\fR,\fIpath\fR,\fInone\fR}
Order of the candidate list; the default is
\fIdate\fR.
\fIdate\fR:
ascending by deletion date, entries without a valid date last.
\fIpath\fR:
ascending by original location, then by date, undated entries last.
\fInone\fR:
keep the trash scan order.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Allow replacing an existing file at the destination; an existing directory
is still never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-dir \fIDIR\fR
Search only
.IR DIR ,
used as\-is and without trust checks.
This option is intentionally absent from
\fB\-\-help\fR
but documented here.
.SH TRASH DIRECTORIES
The trash cans searched are the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty and
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR
otherwise, followed by, for every volume
\fI$volume\fR,
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
and
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR.
\fBTRASH_VOLUMES\fR
is honored, as in
.BR trash\-list (1).
.PP
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
is searched only if
\fB$volume\fB/.Trash\fR
exists, is a directory with the sticky bit, and is not a symlink.
In addition, a searched can is skipped, with a warning on standard error,
when its
.IR info/ " or " files/
subdirectory is a symbolic link or world writable:
\fBTrashDir skipped because its info dir is a symbolic link: \fI<path>\fR
or
\fBTrashDir skipped because its files dir is world writable: \fI<path>\fR.
An unparsable trashinfo file, one that is not valid UTF\-8 included, is
reported on standard error and skipped.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Files restored, an empty reply, or no candidates.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid reply, an overwrite refusal, a restore failure, or an end of
file at the prompt.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Location of the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR.
Used only when set and non\-empty; an empty value is treated as unset, per
the XDG basedir specification, so the home trash falls back to
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
A relative value is used verbatim, resolved against the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Fallback location of the home trash,
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
.TP
.B TRASH_VOLUMES
Colon\-separated volume list overriding the mount table; empty items are
dropped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
The home trash, when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/share/Trash
The home trash otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-restore
trash\-restore /home/user/work
trash\-restore \-\-sort none \-\-overwrite
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trash\-put (1),
.BR trash (1),
.BR trash\-list (1),
.BR trash\-empty (1),
.BR trash\-rm (1).
.PP
Implements the FreeDesktop.org Trash Specification,
https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash\-spec/
